  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nA cold front currently between the I-55 and I-57 corridors in \ncentral IL early this morning will continue to migrate southward \ntoday, likely passing I-70 by noon, but likely stalling out before I-\n64 late today through tonight. Shower and thunderstorm activity will \nfollow the frontal zone, for PoPs decreasing in northern portions of \nthe forecast area today. A disturbance tracking along the front will \nbring another intense period of widespread storm activity to \nsoutheast IL during the morning hours today, then a general lull in \nactivity will take place this afternoon through evening. With 30-40 \nkts of deep layer shear and several hundred J/kg SBCAPE forecast, \nstorms south of I-70 through this afternoon could have the potential \nto produce severe wind gusts to 60 mph. SPC continues a Marginal \nRisk for severe thunderstorms in this area today. Precipitation \namounts will be generally light except for southeast IL, where \namounts around a half inch can be expected in general, although high \nresolution ensembles indicate potential for isolated 2 inch rainfall \nin thunderstorms. WPC continues a Marginal Risk for excessive \nrainfall from around I-70 southward.\n\nOn Thursday, a surface low will will develop along the stalled \nfrontal boundary in the southern Plains early in the morning and \nmove through central IL during the afternoon. This feature should be \ncoincident with 40-50 kts of deep layer shear and 500-1000 J/kg \nMLCAPE by afternoon. This should be sufficient for isolated damaging \nwind gusts and a tornado or two, and SPC has areas near and south of \nI-72 delineated for a Marginal Risk. Rainfall looks to be \ngenerally a half to one inch, but with perhaps a narrow corridor\nor two of 2 inch rainfall accumulations. WPC continues a \nMarginal Risk for excessive rainfall for most of the area.\n\nOnce the low ejects northeast of the area Friday night, a strong \ncold front will sweep through the area, bringing much cooler air to \nthe region and gusty northwest winds. Precipitation will also taper \noff, but a few light showers can't be ruled out Friday as a strong \nupper level shortwave rotates through the area. Following this \nshortwave, several dry days are on track Saturday through at least \nearly next week.\n\nTemperatures today will be down considerably from yesterday's record \nhigh temperatures, with around 67 in Galesburg to around 80 in \nLawrenceville as the front migrates south of the area and cloudy \nand/or rainy conditions suppress temperature rises. Similar highs \nare expected Thursday, before plummeting temperatures arrive for \nFriday. Highs should be mainly between 55 and 60 both Friday and \nSaturday, with lows dipping into the upper 30s both Friday night and \nSaturday night. Models center high pressure to the west Friday \nnight, suggesting that western areas will be more prone to light \nwinds and resulting cold pockets/areas of frost, then the high \nmigrates just to the east Saturday night, suggesting eastern \nareas will be more vulnerable. Temperatures will begin to \nrebound strongly starting Sunday as the high migrates east and \nsoutherly flow begins. Highs should approach 70 Sunday, and \ntrend upward a few degrees per day through the first half of \nnext work week.",
